#{[17592186305072 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$"] [17592186305072 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(12)+1(8) \\\\ 0(12)+3(8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24+8 \\\\ 0+24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305074 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$"] [17592186305074 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}3)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1(10)+1(8) \\\\ 1(10)+1(8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186305244 :answer/type #edb/ref 17592186045499] [17592186305356 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186305433 :answer/type #edb/ref 17592186045499] [17592186305479 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186305622 :answer/type #edb/ref 17592186045499] [17592186305867 :answer/type #edb/ref 17592186045499] [17592186306105 :answer/type #edb/ref 17592186045499] [17592186306280 :answer/type #edb/ref 17592186045499] [17592186306460 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186306475 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186306565 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186306650 :answer/value "nonzero for 